## 6. The Application: Evaluating Existing Law

**Test for Legitimacy:**

| Principle | Question | Pass/Fail |
|:---|:---|:---|
| Fidelity | Does this law serve the human condition? | — |
| Non-Violent Dependence | Is this law grounded in consent? | — |
| Organic Hierarchy | Does this law serve the whole? | — |
| Golden Rule | Does this law treat all equally? | — |
| Acknowledgment | Does this law admit its limits? | — |
| Pursuit | Does this law invite revision? | — |

A law that fails any principle is illegitimate. It must be revised or repealed.

---

## 7. The Implications

**For Authority:**

Authority is a representative—not a master. It must serve the people with fidelity. Fiat authority is illegitimate. Violence is a sign of failure.

**For Law:**

Law is an artifact—it carries loss. It must acknowledge its limits. It must invite correction. It must serve the human condition.

**For the People:**

The people are the principal. They consent to authority. They witness its fidelity. They have the right to revise, resist, and replace illegitimate authority.

**For the Pursuit:**

The pursuit of justice is infinite. There is no final law—only the continued pursuit of fidelity to the human condition.

